WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.500
For some comedians, Saturday Night Live represents
the biggest thing they'll ever do in show

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.000
business.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.080
For whatever reason, their success on late-night
comedy TV never quite transfered to other

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.400
aspects of the entertainment industry, and
they never quite caught that big Hollywood

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:14.900
break.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:20.380
While appearing on Saturday Night Live, Jan
Hooks seemed to do absolutely everything required

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.060
of a future comedy superstar.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.740
Her inspired Betty Davis impersonation is
legendary in its own right.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:32.440
"You wrote a nasty tell-all book and I want
you to have a bad life!

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.540
Hahahahahaha…"

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.140
She played several recurring characters that
instantly endeared her to audiences, including

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.440
her iconic portrayal of Candy Sweeney of the
"Sweeney Sisters" nightclub act.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.040
After leaving SNL in 1991, Hooks never quite
landed a role that showcased her immense talents.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.630
She briefly joined the cast of Designing Women
that same year, replacing Jean Smart.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:59.660
Unfortunately, the show was on its last legs,
and it simply couldn't survive such enormous

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.280
cast changes.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.000
Designing Women ultimately went off the air
in 1993.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.560
She had funny recurring roles on 3rd Rock
from the Sun and 30 Rock and she memorably

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.040
voiced Apu's wife Manjula on The Simpsons.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.040
Still, Hooks didn't find nearly as many projects
as she deserved.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:23.040
When Hooks unexpectedly passed away in 2014
at the age of 57, several SNL legends paid

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.300
homage to the comedian.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.600
Former castmate Kevin Nealon tweeted:

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.520
"My girlfriend in the 80’s and my friend
forever, Janners.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.140
God, were you talented.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.880
Too soon and too sad."

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:39.780
Producer Lorne Michaels let go of most of
the long-standing SNL cast members in 1995,

00:01:39.789 --> 00:01:43.899
bringing in a new slate of performers that
included Will Ferrell, Molly Shannon, and

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:44.520
Darrell Hammond.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.830
On a show that often includes impersonations
of celebrities and political figures, it's

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:53.180
important for SNL to always have a "man of
1,000 voices" in the mix.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.860
From 1995 to 2009, Hammond was that man.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:02.140
A remarkably skilled impressionist, he's probably
the best mimic the show has ever seen.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.560
He took a nuanced, realistic approach, consistently
turning in remarkable impersonations of Bill

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.060
Clinton, Donald Trump, and Sean Connery.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.510
"Sean Connery just pick a catagory"

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.940
"I''ll take horse semen for 800."

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.540
The fact that Hammond disappeared so thoroughly
into his characters may go some way in explaining

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.960
why he never quite got the attention he deserved
even after 14 years in the cast.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.110
He scored a few bit parts in movies like Scary
Movie 3 and landed recurring roles on a few

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:35.240
TV shows like Damages, but he's never been
cast in any particularly monumental roles.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:41.510
In 2014, Hammond rejoined the SNL family as
its announcer, taking over for the deceased

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:42.260
Don Pardo.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.060
Any SNL highlight reel that covers the early
'80s tends to focus on Eddie Murphy's work

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.600
in classic sketches like "Mr. Robinson's Neighborhood."

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.099
But castmate Joe Piscopo was almost as big
of a star, a fact that's often obscured by

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.760
Murphy's tremendous post-SNL success.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:04.080
Piscopo performed a wide range of well-done
impressions on SNL, including Ronald Reagan,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.660
Ricard Nixon, and most famously, Frank Sinatra.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.610
In fact, Murphy and Piscopo teamed up for
a classic sketch parodying the 1982 hit song

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:17.640
"Ebony and Ivory," with Murphy playing Stevie
Wonder, and Piscopo playing Sinatra.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.040
"I'm very into that tune you do with the Beatle
kid, what's his name, uh, the one that looks

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:23.540
like a broad?"

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.740
Piscopo and Murphy appeared on-screen together
a lot in fact, Piscopo played Pokey to Murphy's

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:30.240
Gumby.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.100
But then, in 1984, Piscopo left SNL.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:39.420
That same year, he starred in Johnny Dangerously,
a sendup of 1930s gangster movies.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:41.660
"You shouldn't grab me Johnny.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.740
My mother grabbed me once.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:45.440
Once."

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.880
Piscopo was well-suited to playing tough guys
like Danny Vermin, but the film flopped at

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:51.800
the box office.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:57.020
In fact, it was trounced in its opening weekend
by Eddie Murphy's Beverly Hills Cop.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:02.640
Piscopo's career turned into a string of small
roles in TV shows, along with a few standup

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:08.109
specials for cable TV, an extreme interest
in bodybuilding, and in 2017, floating plans

00:04:08.109 --> 00:04:09.920
to run for governor of New Jersey.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:16.080
As we mentioned earlier, SNL cleaned house
in the fall of 1995, bringing in a bunch of

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.060
new performers to inject some energy into
the aging comedy institution.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.380
Cheri Oteri a veteran of the Los Angeles-based
Groundlings comedy troupe was among them.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:30.900
She brought that live theater dynamic to television
with a collection of truly wild and zany characters.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.620
"You can take your coat off if you'd like."

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.420
"It's from the Burlington Coat Factory."

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.700
Who can forget her psychotically enthusiastic
Spartan cheerleader Arianna?

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:41.860
"Roll call!

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.660
Cha-cha-boochi… roll call!"

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.520
Oh, and what about no-nonsense Nadeen?

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.140
"Simma dahn nah!"

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.980
Oteri was also celebrated for her brilliantly
silly impersonations of Barbara Walters and

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:56.780
Debbie Reynolds.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.670
She often appeared in sketches opposite formidable
co-stars like Will Ferrell and Molly Shannon,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.340
but Oteri always held her own, to say the
least.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.620
She was, without a doubt, one of SNL's brightest
stars until she left the show in 2000.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:15.200
Later that year, she landed the major role
of Gail Hailstorm in the hit Scream sendup

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:15.880
Scary Movie.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:22.100
She went on to star in the unfortunate Dumb
and Dumber prequel Dumb and Dumberer: When

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.220
Harry Met Lloyd.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.960
Since then, she's taken on several guest-starring
gigs and voice roles.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.380
Frankly, she deserves better.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.540
Plenty of SNL stars go big and broad to get
a reaction from the audience but Nora Dunn

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.000
completely disappeared into her roles, much
like a film actress.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:44.840
Between 1985 and 1990, Dunn stepped into several
classic sketches, and she was never anything

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:45.930
less than hilarious.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.980
You might remember her ridiculous turn as
Leona Helmsley.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:55.720
"Right away you'll notice the little things…
things like real skeletons."

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.100
Or how about Babette, her ever-maudlin French
prostitute?

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:02.040
"I'm a woman.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.300
What are you, blind?

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:04.800
Do I have to spell it out for you?

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.340
Woo, woo, woo, W-O-O-O-M-A-N, woman."

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.780
But Dunn's most famous SNL contribution may
have been controversy.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:19.530
She publicly boycotted the second-to-last
episode of the 1989-'90 season, hosted by

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.280
comedian Andrew "Dice" Clay because she found
his material homophobic and abusive to women.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:29.670
In a 1990 interview with Larry King, Clay
basically accused her of doing it all for

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:30.160
publicity:

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:32.520
"You're saying that's for effect, they're
doing it…because they should know it’s

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:33.530
an act?"

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.280
"Yeah, you know, a great example is Nora Dunn."

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.720
More likely, she was simply offended by his
act.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:44.000
Since leaving the show, Dunn has landed a
lot of character-driven work, making appearances

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.480
in shows like Grace and Frankie and It's Always
Sunny in Philadelphia.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:52.460
But with such boundless talent, she deserves
a few plum roles that she can really sink

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:53.140
her teeth into.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:59.160
Ellen Cleghorne was a vital part of the Saturday
Night Live cast of the early '90s, playing

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.280
a long line of memorable characters like Queen
Shenequa.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.000
"Kwanzaa is a Swahili word and it means 'Santa
don't come to my house.'"

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:11.920
Cleghorne left the show of her volition in
1995, when the WB gave the comic performer

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:16.560
her very own sitcom called Cleghorne!, but
it was canceled after just one season.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.520
As far as film work is concerned, Adam Sandler
did cast her in a supporting role in Grown

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:22.080
Ups 2.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.020
Still, she's got a lot going on.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:28.560
After going back to school and earning a PhD
in Performance Studies at New York University's

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.340
Tisch School of the Arts, you should now refer
to her as Dr. Ellen Cleghorne.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:34.300
So there.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.940
Garrett Morris made television history in
1975 as one of the original Saturday Night

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:45.201
Live cast members or, as the group of relative
unknowns was collectively known, "The Not

00:07:45.201 --> 00:07:46.640
Ready for Primetime Players."

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:51.340
Starring on the envelope-pushing series brought
fame and fortune to almost everyone in the

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:54.160
original cast … so why not Garrett Morris?

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.120
During SNL's early years, Morris stood out
for his wild and crazy characters.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:02.520
There was, of course, the Weekend Update translator
for the hard of hearing … a bit that never

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:03.020
gets old.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:09.840
"President Ford is finally over Our top story
tonight … stubborn weeklong cold!"

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:14.380
And fun fact: Morris became the first actor
to ever play Ant-Man on screen.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:18.730
Indeed, he portrays the character in an SNL
sketch called "Superhero Party."

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:20.080
"You don't remember me?

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.520
We met several times.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:23.760
I is Ant-Man."

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.960
Well, that certainly explains Morris's cameo
in Ant-Man, doesn't it?

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:31.820
"What the hell…?"

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.960
While former castmates like Bill Murray and
Dan Aykroyd dominated film comedy in the '80s,

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.340
Morris found the occasional role and appeared
in some rather middling fare.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:44.830
He had a brief cameo in the comedy How to
Beat the High Cost of Living, which co-starred

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.760
fellow SNL alum Jane Curtain.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:53.640
At the age of 74, he was cast as diner cashier
Earl on the long-running 2 Broke Girls, so

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:54.280
there's that.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:58.780
Paul Brittain was 33 when he joined Saturday
Night Live in 2010.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:03.640
He quickly made a name for himself thanks
to some oddball and totally original characters.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:10.060
Why, here comes Lord Cecil Wyndemere, an immature,
old-timey aristocrat who dances around to

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:13.380
harpsichord music and clearly possesses a
pronounced sweet tooth.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:14.940
"This is Cecil."

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:16.920
"I want sweets!"

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:19.100
"What?"

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.220
Don't let his opulent fineries fool you.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:23.740
He's not so different from you and I.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:25.680
You just needed some attention, didn't ya?"

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:26.180
"Yeah."

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:28.180
"I want to hold him."

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.320
And anyone remember this character?

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:33.680
"Croatian import Goran 'Funky Boy' Bogdan."

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.320
"I go to your Sonic restaurant…

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:39.540
I order a plate of dobakcheki … They tell me the
washroom is that way."

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:45.460
During the 2011-12 season, SNL's Lorne Michaels
put Brittain on a half-season contract and

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:49.500
opted not to renew it, making for an abrupt
mid-season disappearance.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:54.460
Since then, Brittain has racked up scarcely
more than a dozen credits, all of them small

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:55.180
character parts.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.580
Probably his most prominent work came in Killing
Gunther, an indie comedy written and directed

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:03.020
by his old SNL pal Taran Killam.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:08.820
Jerry Minor has been a part of numerous innovative
comedy projects and worked with plenty of

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:14.180
comic visionaries, both before and after his
too-short tenure on SNL, which only lasted

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:16.110
through the 2000-2001 season.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:18.220
"Hey, hey!"

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.360
"What is it, Mr. Tarkanian?"

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.140
"Oh, thank you, that's exactly the response
I was looking for."

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:28.200
However, such projects lean more towards the
"cult classic" column than "smash hits."

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:33.680
Prior to SNL, Minor co-starred on David Cross
and Bob Odenkirk's edgy and influential sketch

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.080
comedy series Mr. Show.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:38.280
He was also a correspondent on The Daily Show,
albeit briefly.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.700
Following SNL, Minor starred on the Comedy
Central debate parody Crossballs and had a

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.480
recurring role as Officer Carter on Arrested
Development.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:51.660
He also played an overburdened dad on ABC's
short-lived Carpoolers.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:57.020
More recently, Minor popped up on Unbreakable
Kimmy Schmidt as an inept prosecutor named

00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:02.340
Chris, who just might be based O.J. Simpson
prosecutor Christopher Darden.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:09.240
In 1982, Eddie Murphy hosted SNL while still
in the cast, filling in for his sick 48 Hours

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.480
co-star Nick Nolte.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:16.540
He also changed the show's famous cold open
proclamation, changing it to "Live from New

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.400
York, it's the Eddie Murphy show!"

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.600
And SNL in the early '80s was indeed "The
Eddie Murphy Show."

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:28.779
In fact, the comedy dynamo overshadowed the
solid work of many other cast members including

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:29.779
Tim Kazurinsky.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:31.220
"Your Erroneous Zones.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:32.960
Guy can't even spell."

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:37.500
Here he's interviewing Richard Nixon, played
by Joe Piscopo beneath several pounds of makeup.

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:42.860
"Well, now that you're at the end of your
life, how does it feel to be the most vilified

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:43.820
man in the country?"

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:45.820
"Uh, sensational, I love it."

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:51.540
After leaving SNL in 1984, Kazurinsky hit
Hollywood, which, in the mid-'80s, needed

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.700
guys like him to play nerds and dweebs.

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:57.890
Case in point: He portrayed Carl Sweetchuck
in three Police Academy sequels.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.540
"If you don't like it, you go around me."

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:06.680
Also an SNL writer, Kazurinsky went on to
write scripts for movies and TV, co-writing

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:12.370
the 1986 romantic dramedy About Last Night…,
the 1988 film For Keeps, and writing an episode

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:13.400
of According to Jim.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:18.600
Laura Kightlinger was a force to be reckoned
with on the stand-up comedy circuit in the

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:22.860
'90s, and her self-deprecating wit instantly
won her over with audiences.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:27.870
She eventually landed on Saturday Night LIve
as a double threat working as both a writer

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:31.300
and a performer during the 1994-1995 season.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:34.140
"I bet this has some kind of happy ending,
eh?"

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:36.440
"No, no, Norm — it doesn't."

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:42.300
Indeed, the SNL cast was so overstuffed, she
reportedly struggled to get her material and

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:43.520
her face on the air.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:47.800
After her brief stint with late-night comedy,
Kightlinger returned to stand-up and landed

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:49.920
a bunch of small film and TV roles.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:56.540
In 2006, she emerged with gigs on two prominent
shows: HBO's Lucky Louie and IFC's The Minor

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:58.000
Accomplishments of Jackie Woodman.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:04.100
Kightlinger created the latter, a semi-autobiographical
tale of a self-destructive struggling screenwriter.

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:09.460
Kightlinger still acts on occasion here she
is in a recent episode of Pen15.

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:14.600
"We don't have much time before the show but
as you know, something of Heather's was stolen.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:15.100
A thong."

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:20.780
These days, she's mainly focusing her attention
elsewhere: She's a successful writer and producer

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:24.380
who's worked on Will &amp; Grace a show she's
also appeared in on occasion.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:29.040
Surely all these accomplishments take some
of the sting out of her less-than-legendary

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:30.100
SNL stint.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:31.920
Maybe marriage is in my future."

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.100
".... I certainly envy the lucky guy that
winds up with you, Laura."

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:38.820
"You do, Norm?"

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:39.320
"Yeah."

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:41.540
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:44.620
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
shows are coming soon.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.820
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

